WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.844 --> 00:00:18.985
While Mr. Bumble was proposing to Mrs. Corney, Fagin sat brooding in front of his own fire.

2
00:00:18.986 --> 00:00:23.747
At a table behind him, the Artful Dodger played cards with Charley Bates.

3
00:00:23.748 --> 00:00:30.827
As it was a cold night, the Dodger wore his hat. He and Charley laughed and joked while they played.

4
00:00:30.828 --> 00:00:35.478
"Hark!" the Dodger cried suddenly. "I heard the bell."

5
00:00:35.479 --> 00:00:39.637
Taking the only candle, he crept softly upstairs to open the door.

6
00:00:39.638 --> 00:00:45.057
The bell was rung again with some impatience while Fagin and Charley waited below in darkness.

7
00:00:45.058 --> 00:00:49.287
After a short pause, the Dodger reappeared and whispered to Fagin.

8
00:00:49.288 --> 00:00:53.644
"What!" cried Fagin. "He's alone?"

9
00:00:53.645 --> 00:01:00.293
The Dodger nodded to Fagin. He also signaled to Charley that now would not be a good time for joking.

10
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:04.550
Fagin looked as if he dreaded hearing some terrible news.

11
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.897
"Where is he?" asked Fagin.

12
00:01:06.897 --> 00:01:09.256
The Dodger pointed upstairs.

13
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.701
"Bring him down immediately."

14
00:01:11.701 --> 00:01:17.533
The Dodger went upstairs. He returned, followed by a man wearing a heavy coat.

15
00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:26.434
After a hurried glance around the room, the man pulled off a large scarf that had concealed the lower part of his face. It was Toby Crackit.

16
00:01:26.435 --> 00:01:35.100
"How are you, Fagin?" said Toby. "I can't talk business till I've had something to eat and drink. I've had nothing for three days."

17
00:01:35.101 --> 00:01:41.600
Fagin signaled for the Dodger to bring some food and drink. Then he motioned for Toby to sit at the table.

18
00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:46.330
Once the meal arrived, Fagin dismissed the boys and took a seat at the table.

19
00:01:46.331 --> 00:01:53.679
Toby ate his food slowly, in no hurry to tell his story. Meanwhile Fagin waited in agony.

20
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:59.727
At last Toby was ready to talk. He wiped his mouth and stretched.

21
00:01:59.728 --> 00:02:02.989
"First and foremost, Fagin, how's Bill?"

22
00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:06.568
"What?" screamed Fagin, jumping up from his seat.

23
00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:10.902
"Why, you don't mean to say . . . ," began Toby, turning pale.

24
00:02:10.903 --> 00:02:22.052
"Where are they?" cried Fagin, stamping his foot furiously on the floor. "Sikes and the boy! Where are they hiding? Why have they not been here?"

25
00:02:22.053 --> 00:02:25.465
"The robbery failed," Toby said faintly.

26
00:02:25.466 --> 00:02:33.814
"Yes, I know that." Fagin tore a newspaper page from his pocket and pointed to it. "What else happened?"

27
00:02:33.815 --> 00:02:45.544
"Someone fired a gun and hit the boy. We ran through the fields carrying him between us. They gave chase through hedge and ditch. The whole village was awake, and the dogs were after us."

28
00:02:45.545 --> 00:02:47.428
"And Oliver?"

29
00:02:47.428 --> 00:02:50.886
"His head hung down and he was cold.

30
00:02:50.886 --> 00:03:02.607
They were close on our heels. It was every man for himself to avoid the gallows! Bill and I parted company and left the boy lying in a ditch. Dead or alive, that's all I know about him."

31
00:03:02.608 --> 00:03:08.555
Fagin waited to hear no more. Uttering a yell, he rushed from the room and from the house.

32
00:03:08.555 --> 00:03:11.354
The old man reached the street corner.

33
00:03:11.354 --> 00:03:18.782
He was pressing onward in the same wild manner when suddenly a carriage dashed past and forced him out of the street and onto the pavement.

34
00:03:18.783 --> 00:03:23.258
At last he came to a narrow and dismal alley leading to Saffron Hill.

35
00:03:23.259 --> 00:03:30.845
In its filthy shops were huge bunches of secondhand silk handkerchiefs. They were of all sizes and patterns.

36
00:03:30.846 --> 00:03:35.315
Hundreds of these handkerchiefs dangled from pegs outside the shop windows.

37
00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:39.330
It was into this part of the city that Fagin turned.

38
00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:45.512
He was well known to the citizens of this alley, who were all in the business of buying and selling stolen goods.

39
00:03:45.513 --> 00:03:49.734
They nodded familiarly to him as he passed, but he didn't stop.

40
00:03:49.735 --> 00:03:55.488
Fagin was heading for a certain pub, the one where Bill Sikes and his dog could often be found.

41
00:03:55.489 --> 00:04:00.409
At the pub Fagin made a sign to the man at the bar and walked straight upstairs.

42
00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:04.401
He opened the door to a large, dark room that was filled with people.

43
00:04:04.402 --> 00:04:12.639
Looking around anxiously, he seemed to be in search of some particular person. He beckoned to the landlord and quietly left the room.

44
00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:19.560
"What can I do for you, Mr. Fagin?" inquired the man as he followed him out to the landing. "Won't you join us?"

45
00:04:19.561 --> 00:04:24.102
Fagin shook his head impatiently and whispered, "Is he here?"

46
00:04:24.102 --> 00:04:26.124
"No."

47
00:04:26.124 --> 00:04:28.790
"And no news of Bill Sikes either?"

48
00:04:28.791 --> 00:04:39.957
"None," replied the landlord. "Bill won't stir until it's safe. Depend on that. The police are on the scent down there, and if he moved, he'd blow the whole thing."

49
00:04:39.958 --> 00:04:43.353
"Will he be here tonight?" Fagin asked again.

50
00:04:43.354 --> 00:04:45.211
"Do you mean Monks?"

51
00:04:45.212 --> 00:04:48.877
"Hush," said Fagin. "Yes."

52
00:04:48.878 --> 00:04:53.607
"I expected him before now. If you'll wait ten minutes, he'll probably . . ."

53
00:04:53.608 --> 00:05:00.694
"No, no," said Fagin hastily. "Tell him I was here and that he must come to see me tomorrow night."

54
00:05:00.695 --> 00:05:09.607
With that, Fagin went downstairs and out into the street. He hailed a carriage and rode to within a half mile of Bill Sikes' house.

55
00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:12.601
Then he got out and walked the rest of the way.

56
00:05:12.601 --> 00:05:16.846
He arrived to find no one at Bill Sikes' house but Nancy.

57
00:05:16.846 --> 00:05:22.329
She was sleeping with her head upon the table. She looked up as he closed the door.

58
00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:26.075
"Where do you think Bill is now, my dear?" asked Fagin.

59
00:05:26.076 --> 00:05:30.647
"I can't say," muttered Nancy, laying her head back down on the table.

60
00:05:30.648 --> 00:05:39.980
Fagin told her everything he'd learned from Toby Crackit. "And Oliver. Poor little boy. Left in a ditch, Nancy!"

61
00:05:39.981 --> 00:05:49.803
"The boy is better off where he is," said Nancy, suddenly looking up. "And if no harm comes to Bill from it, I hope Oliver lies dead in that ditch."

62
00:05:49.804 --> 00:05:52.890
"What?" cried Fagin in amazement.

63
00:05:52.891 --> 00:06:07.778
"Yes, I do," said Nancy, meeting his gaze. "I shall be glad to have him out of my sight and to know that the worst is over. I can't bear to have him near me. The sight of him turns me against myself and all of you."

64
00:06:07.779 --> 00:06:13.382
"If Sikes comes back and leaves the boy behind, dead or alive . . ." Fagin said angrily.

65
00:06:13.383 --> 00:06:16.469
"What's all this about?" cried Nancy.

66
00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:26.199
"That boy's worth hundreds of pounds to me," raged Fagin. "And I'm bound to someone, a devil that only wants the will and has the power to . . ."

67
00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:35.858
Suddenly he stopped and his whole demeanor changed. He unclenched his fists and sank quietly into a chair as if afraid that he'd revealed too much.

68
00:06:35.859 --> 00:06:39.914
"Nancy, dear," said Fagin in his usual voice.

69
00:06:39.915 --> 00:06:47.588
"Don't bother me now. I want to sleep. If Toby got away, Bill's pretty sure to be safe too."

70
00:06:47.589 --> 00:06:49.421
"And the boy?"

71
00:06:49.422 --> 00:06:56.478
Briefly Nancy raised her head again. "He'll have to take his chances with the rest of them."
